{N.B. This score, from CHESS Magazine, looks highly improbable towards the end. Possible fixes have been suggested in the notes. Annotations are from CHESS Magazine (where they were unattributed), except for the score-fixing suggestions, which are by John Saunders (BritBase).} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 a6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. g3 Nc6 7. Bg2 Nf6 8. O-O d6 9. Nde2 Be7 10. b3 O-O 11. Bb2 b5 12. Nf4 ({A prophylactic idea, holding up Black's ...d5. Unfortunately, it leaves White equally at a loss for moves. Probably the line played by Ostojic against Suetin in Round 1 (} 12. h3 {, g4 with Ng3 and g5) is better.}) 12... Bb7 13. Re1 Rac8 14. a4 $2 ({Better is} 14. Qd2) 14... b4 15. Na2 a5 16. Rc1 e5 ({Also good are} 16... Qb6 {and 16...Qb8.}) 17. Nd5 Nxd5 18. exd5 Nb8 19. c3 bxc3 ({Here} 19... Bg5 {would have been hard to meet. Now White has the advantage on the queen's side.}) 20. Bxc3 Qb6 21. Qd2 Rc5 22. Rb1 $2 (22. b4 axb4 23. Nxb4 Rfc8 24. a5 {gives a strong advantage. Now White's get jumbled up.}) 22... Qc7 23. b4 axb4 24. Rxb4 Nd7 25. Rb5 Ra8 26. Reb1 Rxb5 27. Rxb5 Rxa4 28. Ba5 Qc8 ({Not} 28... Rxa2 29. Qxa2 Qc1+ 30. Bf1 Ba6 31. Qb1 {or 31 Rb1.}) 29. h4 (29. Bh3 {is not good after} Ba6) 29... Nf6 $2 {A blunder which leads to a forced winning line for White.} 30. Qb2 {A horrible surprise for Black, he now spends twenty minutes recovering from the shock and gets into serious time-trouble.} Ba6 31. Rb8 Rxa5 32. Rxc8+ Bxc8 33. Nb4 Ra8 34. Nc6 Bf8 {Now White's initiative has come a dead halt [sic]; despite his material disadvantage, Black has good winning chances due to the compactness of his position.} 35. Qb1 h5 36. Be4 Nxe4 37. Qxe4 g6 38. Kh2 Kg7 ({See the later note to 39...Bd7 - another possible fix to the score problem would be for Black to play now} 38... Bf5 39. Qe3 {followed by} Kg7 {, as that makes White's 39 Qe3 move a little more logical. JS}) 39. Qe3 {White's only chance is that Black will play ...Ra1 and allow a fork by Nxe5. The ensuing moves by both sides "prepare" the combination.} Bd7 ({Important note (by JS): CHESS magazine clearly gives '39...B-Q2' here but it is apparent from subsequent play that something is radically wrong with the score.} 39... Bf5 {is my suggested fix, after which} 40. Qe2 Ra1 41. Nxe5 Ra5 42. Qd2 Rc5 43. Nf3 Be7 {(draw agreed) looks a far more likely finish. JS}) 40. Qe2 Ra1 41. Nxe5 Ra5 42. Qd2 ({If the score as published were correct, then} 42. Nxd7 {would be an obvious winner here since} Rxd5 {loses horribly to either} 43. Qb2+ {or 44 Qe8. JS}) 42... Rc5 43. Nf3 ({Once again, if the score as published here were correct, then} 43. Nxd7 {would be a very obvious winner here, however short of time the players may have been.}) 43... Be7 {Here a draw was agreed - perhaps Black still has the advantage. Penrose: "I wasn't too impressed with either side's play." Source: CHESS, January 1968, Vol.33/547-8, p138 (with the improbable 39...Bd7 - no other primary sources have yet come to light)} 1/2-1/2

